# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole reactor).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than listing modules: the reactor builds
# `service`'s dependency on `artifacts` from source (a 1.0.0-SNAPSHOT this repo never publishes),
# and a module list would have to be edited every time a module is added. This file is what makes
# that cheap — it drops everything the build does not need, so adding a module stays free and no
# stale output leaks in.

.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage, and ~250 MB of it here
# (service/target alone carries the fast-jar, the native sources dir and the compiled binary).
# Copying it in would also risk a stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# The Angular client's own build output, one level down and for the same two reasons. `npm install`
# runs inside the build stage, and a host node_modules is the worse half: once the webui submodule is
# initialised it is the bulk of the working tree, and its binaries are compiled for the HOST rather
# than for the builder image — Quinoa reuses an existing node_modules instead of reinstalling over
# it, so leaking one in is not merely slow, it is how the image gets a client built by the wrong
# toolchain.
**/node_modules
service/src/main/webui/dist

# Local env / IDE cruft
.env
.idea
.vscode
.settings
.factorypath
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile or a README does not bust the `COPY . .` layer. `docs/openapi.yml` is
# written BY the suite (OpenApiSchemaExportTest), never read by it.
*.md
docs
docker
